# Vast SERVERLESS worker for the InfiniteTalk + CosyVoice pipeline —
# LIGHTWEIGHT + BLACKWELL-ONLY variant (RTX 50-series / B100 / B200, no
# baked-in models).
#
# This file is a direct fork of Dockerfile.lightweight-blackwell — same
# SageAttention compile, same custom-node list and pinned commits, same
# HuggingFace fetch-at-boot model strategy, copied line-for-line. The
# ONLY substantive change is at the very bottom: what the container runs
# when it starts. Everything above the "--- Vast Serverless entrypoint
# ---" marker should be kept in sync BY HAND with Dockerfile.lightweight-
# blackwell if that file changes — same rule that file already applies to
# its own two parents (Dockerfile.lightweight, Dockerfile.blackwell).
#
# STATUS: 2026-08-15, not yet built or tested (no Docker daemon available
# to build with in the session that wrote this). Forked instead of
# layered on top of a separately-built image on purpose, 2026-08-15 —
# an earlier version of this file used `FROM $BASE_IMAGE` to build on top
# of an already-pushed image instead of duplicating the SageAttention/
# model-fetch logic here. That meant this file couldn't be built on its
# own — you'd have to build and push Dockerfile.lightweight-blackwell
# first, as a separate manual step, before this file would even start.
# Every other Dockerfile* in this project is self-contained and buildable
# on its own; this now matches that.
#
# --- What changed from Dockerfile.lightweight-blackwell, and why -------
#
# Dockerfile.lightweight-blackwell's own CMD (`python -u /handler.py`)
# runs handler.py in RunPod's default queue-polling mode — it sits there
# waiting for jobs to arrive from RunPod's own job queue, which a Vast
# Serverless endpoint has no way to push into. Vast Serverless instead
# expects a PyWorker process (worker.py, in this same folder) that
# proxies HTTP requests to a locally-running model server. So this file's
# CMD/ENTRYPOINT run vast-entrypoint.sh instead, which:
#   1. runs fetch-models-entrypoint.sh directly (see vast-entrypoint.sh's
#      own comments for why this is safe to call with no arguments — the
#      short version: its trailing command line becomes a harmless no-op
#      with nothing to run, so this still gets the real HuggingFace fetch
#      without inheriting anything else that line would have done)
#   2. starts handler.py in RunPod's *local hosted-API* mode
#      (--rp_serve_api) instead of its default queue-polling mode
#   3. hands off to Vast's own pyworker bootstrap (start_server.sh,
#      vendored in this folder), which runs worker.py
# See serverless/vast_pyworker/README.md for the full design writeup and
# ../../HANDOFF.md's 2026-08-15 "Vast Serverless" sections for how this
# was arrived at and tested (without real GPU hardware — see "Not yet
# verified" below).
#
# worker.py, requirements.txt, and benchmark_payload.json are NOT baked
# into this image — they're cloned at container BOOT (not build) time by
# start_server.sh, from whichever git repo PYWORKER_REPO points at on the
# Vast endpoint/template config. Push this folder's contents there
# separately; see the README's "Setup steps" §1.
#
# --- A real, unresolved risk worth reading before deploying this -------
#
# `auto-fetch-models.sh` (in this project, wired in below the same as
# Dockerfile.lightweight-blackwell) exists because of something already
# confirmed on real rented Vast hardware, 5 boxes in a row, 2026-08-14:
# a custom Docker image's own ENTRYPOINT does not reliably run when Vast
# deploys it. That finding is from Vast's regular on-demand GPU rental
# flow (rent a box, SSH in) — auto-fetch-models.sh's fix for it is an
# SSH-login trigger (/etc/profile.d), which has no equivalent for a Vast
# SERVERLESS worker: nothing logs in, there's no shell to fire from.
#
# UPDATE, 2026-08-15, after actually reading Vast's own docs (not just
# inferring from a code comment — see HANDOFF.md for the full citation
# list): the "ENTRYPOINT doesn't run" finding is explained, in Vast's own
# words. docs.vast.ai/instances/launch-modes and
# docs.vast.ai/documentation/templates/template-settings both confirm
# regular instances have a per-template Launch Mode: "Docker ENTRYPOINT"
# mode runs the image exactly as built; "SSH"/"Jupyter" modes explicitly
# state "the docker entrypoint for your image will not be run — it will
# be replaced with our instance setup script." That's an intentional,
# documented platform behavior, not an undocumented quirk — the 5/5-boxes
# finding is almost certainly boxes that were on SSH or Jupyter launch
# mode (the default/common choice for an interactive rental), not a
# platform-wide inability to run ENTRYPOINT at all.
#
# For Serverless specifically: docs.vast.ai/guides/serverless/
# creating-new-pyworkers.md describes the worker boot sequence as "the
# start-server script (provided by the template)" doing exactly four
# things — clone PYWORKER_REPO, install requirements.txt, start the model
# server, run worker.py — which is precisely what vast-entrypoint.sh +
# the vendored start_server.sh already do here. That page never mentions
# ENTRYPOINT, CMD, or Launch Mode at all (checked verbatim, not just
# summarized) — Serverless templates don't appear to expose the
# Jupyter/SSH/ENTRYPOINT selector regular instances have, which is
# consistent with (though not an explicit statement of) Serverless always
# running the image as declared. This is now well-supported by primary
# sources, not just plausible inference — but no page states outright
# "Serverless always honors ENTRYPOINT," so it's still worth checking on
# the first real deploy: does vast-entrypoint.sh's first log line
# ("[vast-entrypoint] fetch-at-boot image detected...") ever show up? If
# it doesn't, come back to this comment and HANDOFF.md before spending
# more build time.
#
# Separately noted, not yet acted on: Vast also documents a newer,
# Python-SDK-based Serverless deployment path (app.image()/.run_script()/
# app.ensure_ready(), see docs.vast.ai/guides/serverless/deployments/
# configuration.md) that sidesteps hand-written Dockerfiles entirely. This
# project still uses the classic PYWORKER_REPO + template-env-var path
# vast-ai/pyworker's own README documents — that path is still current and
# documented, just not the only one Vast now offers. Not investigated
# further; flagging in case it's worth revisiting.

FROM nextdiffusionai/comfyui-sageattention:cuda12.8-v1

# Base image doesn't include ffmpeg — required by VHS_VideoCombine and for
# duration measurement. (Same fix as every other Dockerfile in this repo.)
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends ffmpeg \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# Same absolute path as the pod (/workspace/ComfyUI) — matches
# handler.py's default and keeps every relative path below short.
WORKDIR /workspace/ComfyUI

# --- ComfyUI core: pinned to the exact commit confirmed on the pod -------
RUN git clone https://github.com/comfyanonymous/ComfyUI.git . \
 && git checkout b7ba504e0614f81549f7b05cece271ed5d91bc9a \
 && python -m pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

# --- SageAttention: compiled from source, baked into image layers ---------
# Same pre-flight rationale as every other Dockerfile here — see
# Dockerfile's extensive comments for the full story, not duplicated here.
RUN python -c "\
import torch, subprocess; \
print('=== SageAttention build pre-flight ==='); \
print('torch.__version__      :', torch.__version__); \
print('torch.version.cuda     :', torch.version.cuda); \
from torch.utils.cpp_extension import CUDA_HOME; \
print('CUDA_HOME (build)      :', CUDA_HOME); \
assert torch.version.cuda is not None, 'torch is a CPU-only build -- nothing to compile SageAttention against.'; \
assert CUDA_HOME is not None, 'CUDA_HOME not found -- base image only ships CUDA runtime libs, not the build toolkit.'; \
subprocess.run([CUDA_HOME + '/bin/nvcc', '--version'], check=True); \
"

# --- The one line that makes this file Blackwell-only instead of Ampere+Ada
# Blackwell only, deliberately alone — same reasoning as Dockerfile.blackwell
# (SageAttention's Hopper kernel + untested arch-combination risk, see that
# file's header comment for the full story, not repeated here).
ENV TORCH_CUDA_ARCH_LIST="12.0"
ENV EXT_PARALLEL=1
# MAX_JOBS=2, not Dockerfile.lightweight's 1 — genuine single-architecture
# compile here, same shape as Dockerfile.blackwell's proven single-arch
# build (~24 min at MAX_JOBS=2 for the equivalent 8.9 test, per
# Dockerfile.sageattention-test's history). Drop to 1 first if this OOMs
# before assuming anything more exotic is wrong.
RUN python -m pip install --no-cache-dir ninja \
 && git clone https://github.com/thu-ml/SageAttention.git /tmp/SageAttention \
 && git -C /tmp/SageAttention checkout d1a57a546c3d395b1ffcbeecc66d81db76f3b4b5 \
 && MAX_JOBS=2 python -m pip install --no-cache-dir --no-build-isolation /tmp/SageAttention \
 && rm -rf /tmp/SageAttention \
 && python -c "import sageattention; print('sageattention import OK:', sageattention.__file__)"

RUN mkdir -p custom_nodes

# --- comfyui-manager: pinned to the exact commit confirmed on the pod ----
RUN git clone https://github.com/ltdrdata/ComfyUI-Manager.git custom_nodes/comfyui-manager \
 && git -C custom_nodes/comfyui-manager checkout ab8ce7814aa1cd4f9a88b7b0313f64395f132972 \
 && python -m pip install --no-cache-dir -r custom_nodes/comfyui-manager/requirements.txt


# --- The five nodes that had no pod-side git metadata: pinned below to
# the exact commits recovered from the last known-good :v2 image on
# ghcr.io (docker pull :v2 + git rev-parse HEAD inside each
# custom_nodes/<repo> dir), after a full local Docker cache wipe caused an
# unpinned rebuild to silently pull fresh main HEAD for all five and broke
# MultiTalkWav2VecEmbeds (NoneType error) on :v3. 2026-08-26.
RUN git clone https://github.com/kijai/ComfyUI-WanVideoWrapper.git custom_nodes/ComfyUI-WanVideoWrapper \
 && git -C custom_nodes/ComfyUI-WanVideoWrapper checkout 088128b224242e110d3906c6750e9a3a348a659b \
 && python -m pip install --no-cache-dir -r custom_nodes/ComfyUI-WanVideoWrapper/requirements.txt

# --- TTS node packs REMOVED 2026-09-15 -------------------------------
# TTS-Audio-Suite and comfyui_fl-cosyvoice3 used to be cloned here. TTS
# left ComfyUI when CosyVoice was retired for Fish Speech S2-Pro on its
# own always-on Vast instance (see ../../vast image/README.md); this
# image now receives a finished mp3 and only animates it. Removing them
# also removes the models/TTS/CosyVoice symlink problem entirely.
# MelBandRoFormer stays -- vocal isolation for lipsync still runs here.
RUN git clone https://github.com/kijai/ComfyUI-MelBandRoFormer.git custom_nodes/ComfyUI-MelBandRoFormer \
 && git -C custom_nodes/ComfyUI-MelBandRoFormer checkout 92c86854e6654f4aacc97484471af95c98ea16d4 \
 && python -m pip install --no-cache-dir -r custom_nodes/ComfyUI-MelBandRoFormer/requirements.txt


RUN git clone https://github.com/kijai/ComfyUI-KJNodes.git custom_nodes/comfyui-kjnodes \
 && git -C custom_nodes/comfyui-kjnodes checkout 3f20054214fec9f9234fd3841ae6f1e4287948f6 \
 && python -m pip install --no-cache-dir -r custom_nodes/comfyui-kjnodes/requirements.txt

RUN git clone https://github.com/Kosinkadink/ComfyUI-VideoHelperSuite.git custom_nodes/comfyui-videohelpersuite \
 && git -C custom_nodes/comfyui-videohelpersuite checkout 4ee72c065db22c9d96c2427954dc69e7b908444b \
 && python -m pip install --no-cache-dir -r custom_nodes/comfyui-videohelpersuite/requirements.txt

# This worker is headless and API-driven — it never uses ComfyUI-Manager's
# UI to install nodes at runtime — so its startup registry fetch is pure
# waste. Confirmed on RunPod: ~2 minutes of every cold start.
RUN mkdir -p user/__manager && \
    printf '[default]\nnetwork_mode = offline\n' > user/__manager/config.ini

# --- transformers: pinned to the exact version confirmed working on :v2 ---
# None of the custom nodes above pin their own `transformers` requirement,
# so a genuinely fresh build (cache wiped, no layer to reuse) resolves it to
# whatever's newest on PyPI at that moment. Confirmed drift: 5.15.0 (:v2,
# working) -> 5.16.1 (:v4, broke MultiTalkWav2VecEmbeds with a NoneType
# error) despite identical custom-node commits and identical torch/
# torchaudio (those come from the base image, unaffected). This RUN must
# stay AFTER all custom-node installs above so it overrides whatever
# version any of their requirements.txt pulled in. 2026-08-27.
RUN python -m pip install --no-cache-dir "transformers==5.15.0"

# --- Model weights: NOT baked in, on purpose -------------------------------
# Same as Dockerfile.lightweight — this is the entire point of the
# "lightweight" half of this file's name. No COPY of ComfyUI-models
# anywhere; fetch-models.py pulls everything from HuggingFace at boot.
#
# Same correction as Dockerfile.lightweight, 2026-08-13: no `[hf_transfer]`
# extra / HF_HUB_ENABLE_HF_TRANSFER here anymore -- that's dead, the Hub is
# fully Xet-backed now and hf-xet ships as a hard dependency on its own.
# HF_XET_HIGH_PERFORMANCE=1 baked in below, same as Dockerfile.lightweight
# -- confirmed 2026-08-13 that Vast's rentable fleet (5090/B100/B200 for
# this file specifically) isn't RAM-starved at the 64GB+ level this flag
# wants. See Dockerfile.lightweight's comment at this same spot for the
# full citation, and README's "Maximizing HF-fetch speed" section for
# HF_TOKEN, which stays a runtime-only env var, not baked in here either.
RUN python -m pip install --no-cache-dir "huggingface_hub>=0.32.0"
ENV HF_XET_HIGH_PERFORMANCE=1

RUN pip install --no-cache-dir runpod boto3

COPY handler.py /handler.py
COPY workflow_api.json /workflow_api.json

# --- 1080p upscale ----------------------------------------------------------
# upscale.py must sit next to /handler.py -- sys.path[0] is / for
# `python -u /handler.py`, which is how `import upscale` resolves.
COPY upscale.py /upscale.py
# Weights baked in (2.4MB): realesr-animevideov3, Real-ESRGAN, BSD-3-Clause.
# Image-local rather than under COMFY_ROOT on purpose -- that is the shared
# network volume and concurrent workers would race writing it. Fetched with
# python (no curl/wget in this image) and hash-checked here so a changed
# upstream file fails the BUILD rather than shipping silently.
RUN mkdir -p /opt/upscale && python -c "\
import hashlib, sys, urllib.request; \
u='https://github.com/xinntao/Real-ESRGAN/releases/download/v0.2.5.0/realesr-animevideov3.pth'; \
p='/opt/upscale/realesr-animevideov3.pth'; \
urllib.request.urlretrieve(u, p); \
h=hashlib.sha256(open(p,'rb').read()).hexdigest(); \
want='b8a8376811077954d82ca3fcf476f1ac3da3e8a68a4f4d71363008000a18b75d'; \
sys.exit('upscale weights sha256 mismatch: '+h) if h!=want else print('upscale weights ok', h)"

ENV COMFY_ROOT=/workspace/ComfyUI
ENV COMFY_SKIP_REQ_SYNC=1

# --- Boot-time model fetch + per-line timing ---------------------------
# Identical wiring to Dockerfile.lightweight-blackwell: fetch-models.py
# downloads every model from HuggingFace, log-timestamps.py gives every
# boot per-line elapsed-time numbers — see README's "Measuring every
# step" section. vast-entrypoint.sh (below) calls fetch-models-
# entrypoint.sh directly rather than relying on it as ENTRYPOINT, so this
# COPY is still needed but the file is invoked differently than in the
# parent Dockerfile.
COPY fetch-models.py /fetch-models.py
COPY fetch-models-entrypoint.sh /fetch-models-entrypoint.sh
RUN chmod +x /fetch-models-entrypoint.sh
COPY log-timestamps.py /log-timestamps.py

# auto-fetch-models.sh (SSH-login fallback) is NOT copied into this image
# — it fires from /etc/profile.d on an interactive login shell, and a
# Vast Serverless worker has no login shell for anything to fire from.
# See the "real, unresolved risk" comment near the top of this file for
# why that fallback existing at all for the parent image is worth reading
# before deploying this one.

# --- Vast Serverless entrypoint -----------------------------------------
# Only lines in this file that differ from Dockerfile.lightweight-
# blackwell's own ENTRYPOINT/CMD. See vast-entrypoint.sh's own comments
# for the full boot sequence it runs.
COPY serverless/vast_pyworker/start_server.sh /start_server.sh
COPY serverless/vast_pyworker/vast-entrypoint.sh /vast-entrypoint.sh
# Boot forensics: boot counter (restart vs recreate), cgroup OOM-kill counter,
# and a background memory sampler whose previous-run tail is replayed at the
# next boot -- the only way to see the seconds before a silent container death,
# since serverless workers can't be SSH'd into and the reboot resets stdout.
# Invoked from vast-entrypoint.sh, guarded so it can never fail a boot.
COPY serverless/vast_pyworker/boot-forensics.sh /boot-forensics.sh
# router.py: the single address PyWorker forwards to (:8000), fanning out to
# one handler.py + one ComfyUI per GPU. Runs for 1-GPU boxes too -- one code
# path. Stdlib only, no extra pip install. 2026-08-30.
COPY serverless/vast_pyworker/router.py /router.py
RUN chmod +x /start_server.sh /vast-entrypoint.sh /boot-forensics.sh /router.py

ENTRYPOINT ["/vast-entrypoint.sh"]
CMD []
